<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy | Aging at Home Lab" description="Aging at Home Lab editorial policy, sourcing standards, safety boundaries, and correction principles.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Editorial Policy</p>
    <h1>Trust is the product.</h1>
    <p>Aging at Home Lab covers topics that can affect health, safety, independence, money, legal planning, and family decision-making. Our editorial standards are designed to keep the work practical, transparent, and appropriately cautious.</p>

    <h2>What every guide should do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Answer a real caregiver or aging-in-place problem.</li>
      <li>Give the reader a practical next step.</li>
      <li>Use credible sources for factual claims.</li>
      <li>Explain when to call a qualified professional.</li>
      <li>Avoid fear-based selling or exaggerated safety promises.</li>
      <li>Disclose affiliate or sponsored relationships clearly when present.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Source standards</h2>
    <p>For safety, health, legal, financial, or public-statistic claims, we prioritize government, nonprofit, professional, academic, manufacturer, or primary-source documentation. User reviews and forums may help us understand reader pain points, but they are not treated as safety or medical evidence.</p>

    <h2>Expert review</h2>
    <p>Some topics should be reviewed by a relevant professional before being treated as final guidance. These may include medication management, fall-risk assessment, dementia-related safety, bed rails, mobility aids, major home modifications, legal documents, financial benefits, or discharge planning.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>If we discover an error, outdated claim, unsafe implication, or unclear disclosure, the content should be updated and marked with a new review date where appropriate.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
